WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.060
- So we joke about robots
taking over, but they're really

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.630
helpful for police, so
what's the big deal?

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.830
Well, let's take a look.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.230
And there is the robot.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.385
That looks like your classic
Boston Dynamics robot.

00:00:13.385 --> 00:00:16.880
- I've never seen nothing
like this before in my life.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.604
- Yeah, me neither. I've
never seen that either.

00:00:19.604 --> 00:00:21.420
- Oh, that (bleep)
walks better than a dog.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.160
- Somehow it doesn't look
as cute when it's dressed up

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.010
as a police officer and police livery.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.940
And this raises all kinds
of fourth amendment issues.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.090
According to the Supreme
court in Terry vs Ohio,

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.860
the police are allowed to detain

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.240
and search people if there
is a reasonable suspicion.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.340
But what does that mean
in the context of robots.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.340
Who might have sensors
that are far more sensitive

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.670
than humans.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.830
They might be just patrolling around.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.200
Does that give them the
ability to stop people

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.840
just using a remote-controlled robot?

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.330
Does it infringe on
constitutional liberties?

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.760
This is not an issue
that is going to go away.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.690
Police departments are
probably going to use more

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.553
and more robots, but we'll
see what the courts say.

